<Docs slug="harnesses">
  <h1>Harnesses</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Claude Code, Codex, Pi, OpenCode, and Kiro CLI get live status indicators and learn the <code>ouijit</code>
    CLI automatically. No configuration required.
  </p>

  <h2>What you get</h2>
  <p>Run <code>claude</code>, <code>codex</code>, <code>pi</code>, <code>opencode</code>, or <code>kiro-cli</code> in any Ouijit terminal and two things happen:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Live status</strong>. The terminal card shows whether the agent is thinking or idle, visible
      across the whole stack. You can tell which sessions need attention without bringing each one forward.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>CLI awareness</strong>. The agent knows the <a href="/docs/cli/"><code>ouijit</code> CLI</a>, so
      it can create tasks, advance the board, stage review comments, and open panels from the shell. See
      <a href="/docs/agents/">Agents</a>.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    This applies only in terminals Ouijit opens, including <a href="/docs/sandbox/">sandboxed</a> ones.
    Everywhere else these tools behave as they always do.
  </p>

  <h2>Kiro CLI</h2>
  <p>
    Ouijit installs an <code>ouijit</code> agent for Kiro (<code>~/.kiro/agents/ouijit.json</code>) carrying the
    status hooks and the CLI reference, and a wrapper that selects it. Kiro's CLI 3.0 early-access engine is
    opt-in per invocation: run <code>kiro-cli --v3</code> yourself, or set <code>OUIJIT_KIRO_V3=1</code> in a hook
    command's environment to have the wrapper add the flag. On v3 the status hooks come from a global hooks file
    under <code>~/.kiro/hooks/</code>; the CLI reference is v2-only for now.
  </p>

  <h2>Notifications</h2>
  <p>
    When an agent finishes a turn, Ouijit plays a sound. If the window isn't focused, you also get an OS
    notification. Turn the sound off under <strong>App Settings &rarr; Sound</strong>.
  </p>
</Docs>
